    summary_en: "UNAM, Mexico's largest university, recently implemented an AI proctoring and remote examination system for its university entrance exams. However, the exam results showed an abnormal surge in data, particularly in the proportion of top scores, which was significantly higher compared to data from previous years (2021 to 2025). This raised academic concerns regarding large-scale cheating. To address the issue of academic fairness, UNAM established an expert committee to investigate. The committee ultimately recommended that all affected students, including those who have already been granted admission spots this year, must participate in a 'control exam.' It is estimated that approximately 58,000 students will be affected and must retake the exam to determine their final admission status. The university president apologized to honest students but emphasized that this retake is necessary to ensure the certainty and fairness of the admission process.",
